All three statements return the same numeric result, but 2result.sql is the safest and simplest:

• It joins only the tables that are strictly needed (rental → inventory → film_category → category → customer / address / city).  
• No unnecessary extra join to the film table (as in 0result.sql), so there is zero risk of multiplying rows if a film ever had more than one row in film (extended editions, etc.).  
• It avoids database‑specific quoted identifiers (used heavily in 1result.sql), keeping the query portable across engines.  
• Its WHERE clause exactly matches the spec, while 1result.sql mixes case handling (UPPER on one pattern, not on the other), which could miss some mixed‑case “‑” city names in a case‑sensitive collation.

Therefore, 2result.sql is the best choice.

```plaintext
2result.sql
```